course: dictionary
lesson: 15375
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them give you clues about which meaning the writer wants. Look at what the people are doing. «The boxers were sparring in the gym, wearing gloves and head guards.» The gym, the gloves and the head guards tell you sparring means practice fighting, using light hits to train.
explain: «The twins were sparring about whose turn it was on the games console, trading quick, funny remarks.» Nobody is fighting here. The words trading remarks tell you sparring means arguing in a friendly, not very serious way. Ask yourself: what is happening, and who is doing it?
explain: Sparring can also be a verb. Spar, spars, sparred and sparring all come from the same word. «Sam sparred with his coach for ten minutes.» means Sam practised fighting with his coach. Try your meaning in the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Sparring means practice fighting with a partner, using light hits and safety gear, to train and not to hurt.
fact: Sparring can also mean arguing in a friendly or not very serious way.
fact: To spar means to practise fighting, or to argue with someone.
fact: A «sparring partner» is someone you train or argue with often.
fact: A «sparring session» is a set time of practice, such as in a karate or taekwondo class.
exercise: «The boxers were sparring in the gym, wearing gloves and head guards.» What is sparring in this sentence? | Running fast around the track | Winning a big competition | *Practice fighting with a partner, using light hits to train | A kind of gym shoe
exercise: «Kira and her brother were sparring about whose turn it was on the games console.» What is sparring here? | *Arguing in a friendly, not very serious way | Sleeping on the sofa | Crying loudly at bedtime | Practice fighting with gloves and head guards
exercise: Lee says, «My sparring partner is coming round after school.» What does sparring partner mean? | Someone who wins every race | *Someone you train or argue with often | A dog that likes to run | A teacher who marks your work
test: What does the verb spar mean? | To hide under a bed | To eat a big dinner | To swim across a lake | *To practise fighting, or to argue with someone
test: «The sparring was friendly, and both of them laughed at the end.» What kind of sparring is this? | A dance lesson in the hall | Painting pictures in class | Hard fighting with no rules | *Friendly arguing with quick, clever remarks
exam: «The coach said sparring is for training, not for hurting each other.» What is the main idea? | Sparring is a type of dance | Sparring is only for adults | Sparring means shouting at each other | *Sparring is practice fighting to train, not to hurt
